import pf.Html
import ../../../Demos/AdminPreview
import ../../../Ui/Callout
import ../../../Ui/Code
import ../../../Ui/Heading
import ../../../Ui/Note
import ../../../Generated/Sources
import ../../../Generated/Paths
import ../../../Ui/PageHero
import ../../../Ui/TableWrap

## The admin package: what it is for, a first admin (code the site's server compiles), its pages as
## the package renders them (from made-up tables, while this page renders), who may use it, its
## filters, options and widgets, how it works, its modules, and what works today. All of it is static: the
## browser runs nothing here.
meta = {
	title: "Admin",
	description: "A Django-style admin for a Roc Solid app's Postgres tables: each table's rows with a search, a filter in every column, sorting and pages, and a form for each row, read from Postgres's own catalog.",
	toc: [
		{ id: "first", label: "A first admin" },
		{ id: "looks", label: "What it looks like" },
		{ id: "pages", label: "Its pages" },
		{ id: "filters", label: "Filters" },
		{ id: "access", label: "Who may use it" },
		{ id: "options", label: "Options" },
		{ id: "widgets", label: "Widgets" },
		{ id: "how", label: "How it works" },
		{ id: "modules", label: "The modules" },
		{ id: "status", label: "Status" },
	],
}

view! = |_|
	<>
		<NoHydration>
			<PageHero
				kicker={[<>{"The ecosystem · "}</>, <code>packages/admin</code>]}
				title="An admin for your tables, read from Postgres itself"
			>
				Django's admin, for a Roc Solid app: a page for each table, its rows with a search, a filter in
				every column, sorting and pages, a form for each row, and a way to add and delete rows. It reads the tables from
				Postgres's own catalog when a page is asked for, so there is nothing to generate, and nothing to keep
				in step with the schema. It is mounted as its <a href={"${Paths.ecosystem_jobs}#page"}>jobs page</a>
				is: one route file, behind the app's own middleware.
			</PageHero>

			<Heading id="first">A first admin</Heading>
			<p>Name the package in the app's header, by its directory's name, as this site names it:</p>
			<Code title={Sources.admin_header.file} source={Sources.admin_header.rocx} numbers={Bool.False} />
			<p>
				Then a route file at the path the admin is to have answers every request there with
				<code>AdminPage.respond!</code>. It names its middleware, which says who is asking
				(<code>middleware = Middlewares.staff</code>), and the admin's options: only <code>mount</code> and
				<code>access</code> are needed, and only a table's <code>name</code>.
			</p>
			<Code title={Sources.admin_route.file} source={Sources.admin_route.rocx} />
			<p>
				In an app, the three definitions are a route file's (<code>routes/admin/db/+server.roc</code>). Here
				they are a module of this site, so the compiler checks them with the site's server; nothing answers
				with them, since the site has no database.
			</p>
			<Callout kind="note" title="Why the app hands it Db.all!">
				<p>
					A Roc package can't import a platform's modules: it may name a platform only if it is the app's own,
					and a Roc Solid app is two programs on two platforms. So the app passes the three effects the
					admin runs on, the server platform's <code>Db.all!</code>, <code>Db.run!</code> and
					<code>Random.bytes!</code>, and the compiler checks that they fit. The browser's program never
					compiles the admin: it doesn't import a <code>+server.roc</code>.
				</p>
			</Callout>

			<Heading id="looks">What it looks like</Heading>
			<p>
				These are the admin's pages as the package renders them, by its own <code>View</code>, while this page
				renders: from a made-up catalog of two tables, <code>books</code> and <code>authors</code>, with the
				options above. Nothing asks a database, and the frames are sandboxed, so their links and forms do
				nothing here. They are drawn in the platform's admin look, as the <a href="/platform/jobs">jobs page</a>
				is: the tables in a sidebar (across the top when the frame is narrow), a header with the page's
				actions, panels, and light or dark as your system is.
			</p>
			<figure class="admin-preview">
				<iframe class="list" title="The admin's list of books, as the package renders it" srcdoc={AdminPreview.list_page} sandbox="" loading="lazy"></iframe>
				<figcaption>
					A table's rows, filtered by <code>price</code> from 10: under each heading, a filter from its column's
					type (a range of numbers, the text a title contains, the value a foreign key is, a select of the
					check's three values for <code>state</code>), sent by Enter or the Filter button. A heading sorts by its
					column, the search looks in <code>title</code> and <code>blurb</code>, and a foreign key links to its row.
				</figcaption>
			</figure>
			<figure class="admin-preview">
				<iframe title="The admin's form for one book, as the package renders it" srcdoc={AdminPreview.row_page} sandbox="" loading="lazy"></iframe>
				<figcaption>
					A row's form, just saved: a select of authors for the foreign key, of the check's values for
					<code>state</code>, a checkbox, a decimal's input, a moment in UTC, and a text area for text of several
					lines. The key is shown, not written.
				</figcaption>
			</figure>
			<details>
				<summary>The made-up tables, and the pages rendered from them: <code>{Sources.admin_preview.file}</code></summary>
				<Code title={Sources.admin_preview.file} source={Sources.admin_preview.rocx} />
			</details>

			<Heading id="pages">Its pages</Heading>
			<p>
				Every page is the admin's path and a query, so one route file is all it takes. A query names a table
				and a column only among those the app lets it show.
			</p>
			<TableWrap>
				<table>
					<thead><tr><th>Query</th><th>Page</th></tr></thead>
					<tbody>
						<tr><td>(none)</td><td>the tables, each with about how many rows it has (Postgres's estimate), its comment, and a link to add a row</td></tr>
						<tr>
							<td><code>?table=books</code></td>
							<td>
								its rows, 50 a page (<code>page=2</code>), counted and paged in Postgres; a search
								(<code>q=cloud</code>), a filter in each column (<code>has.title=cloud</code>,
								<code>from.price=10</code>, <code>f.state=draft</code>, <code>is.author_id=null</code>: see
								<a href="#filters">Filters</a>), and a sort (<code>sort=title</code>, <code>sort=-price</code>)
							</td>
						</tr>
						<tr><td><code>?table=books&amp;k.id=21</code></td><td>a row's form: <code>k.</code> and a column for each column of its primary key, so a key of two columns works too</td></tr>
						<tr><td><code>?table=books&amp;new</code></td><td>a new row's form, its constant defaults filled in (<code>true</code>, <code>'draft'</code>, <code>0</code>)</td></tr>
						<tr><td><code>?table=books&amp;k.id=21&amp;delete</code></td><td>the question before a row is deleted, and the button that deletes it</td></tr>
					</tbody>
				</table>
			</TableWrap>
			<p>
				A form sent adds, changes or deletes the row, then goes on to its page, which says so (Saved.). An
				update writes only the columns that changed. When Postgres refuses (a price that isn't a number, an
				author that books still name), the form comes back with what was sent and Postgres's reason. A table
				without a primary key is listed, not opened.
			</p>

			<Heading id="filters">Filters</Heading>
			<p>
				Each column a list shows has a filter, in a row under the column headings, chosen from its type; a hidden
				column never has one. Filtering a column the list shows tells a visitor nothing they can't see, so there
				is no option for it: to filter by a column, list it.
			</p>
			<TableWrap>
				<table>
					<thead><tr><th>Column</th><th>Filter</th><th>Query</th></tr></thead>
					<tbody>
						<tr><td><code>text</code>, <code>varchar</code>, <code>uuid</code>, <code>json</code> and any other type</td><td>the text it contains, in any case; <code>%</code> and <code>_</code> match themselves</td><td><code>has.title=cloud</code></td></tr>
						<tr><td>a number: <code>integer</code>, <code>bigint</code>, <code>numeric</code>, <code>real</code>, …</td><td>from, to, or both, inclusive</td><td><code>from.price=10&amp;to.price=20</code></td></tr>
						<tr><td><code>date</code>, <code>timestamp</code>, <code>timestamptz</code> (in UTC), <code>time</code></td><td>from, to, or both, inclusive, with the browser's pickers</td><td><code>from.published_at=2026-03-01T00:00</code></td></tr>
						<tr><td><code>boolean</code></td><td>a select: any, yes, no</td><td><code>f.in_print=true</code></td></tr>
						<tr><td>an enum, or a check that it is one of a few values</td><td>a select of its values</td><td><code>f.state=draft</code></td></tr>
						<tr><td>a foreign key</td><td>the value it is</td><td><code>f.author_id=3</code></td></tr>
						<tr><td>any of these, if it may be null</td><td>empty and not empty too: in its select, or a small select under its inputs</td><td><code>is.blurb=null</code>, <code>is.blurb=set</code></td></tr>
					</tbody>
				</table>
			</TableWrap>
			<p>
				The filters are one GET form, with no JavaScript: Enter in any of them sends it, or the Filter button
				beside the search. It keeps the search and the sort and goes back to the first page, and the pages' links
				and the sort keep the filters. A filter's value is checked in Roc before it reaches SQL (a number for a
				number, <code>2026-03-14</code> for a date, one of an enum's labels), then a range's is cast to its
				column's type (<code>{"\"price\" >= $3::numeric"}</code>). A value that isn't one is ignored, and its
				filter says so (Ignored: not a number): the page is never an error.
			</p>

			<Heading id="access">Who may use it</Heading>
			<p>
				The route's middleware runs first, as for any route: it can send a stranger to sign in. Then
				<code>access</code> decides, from the context the middleware handed on:
			</p>
			<TableWrap>
				<table>
					<thead><tr><th><code>access</code> says</th><th>Then</th></tr></thead>
					<tbody>
						<tr><td><code>Edit</code></td><td>every page, and adding, changing and deleting rows</td></tr>
						<tr><td><code>ReadOnly</code></td><td>the tables, rows and values; no forms</td></tr>
						<tr><td><code>Refused</code></td><td>a 403, and nothing is read</td></tr>
					</tbody>
				</table>
			</TableWrap>
			<p>
				A form's POST must come from a page of the same site (its <code>Origin</code>) and send back the token its
				page was given, which the admin keeps in a cookie (<code>rocx_admin_csrf</code>: for the admin's path
				only, out of scripts' reach, never sent with another site's form). Anything else is refused with a 403
				before a table is read.
			</p>

			<Heading id="options">Options</Heading>
			<p><code>Admin.Config(context)</code>, the admin:</p>
			<TableWrap>
				<table>
					<thead><tr><th>Field</th><th>Is</th></tr></thead>
					<tbody>
						<tr><td><code>mount : Str</code></td><td>the route's path</td></tr>
						<tr><td><code>{"access : context -> [Edit, ReadOnly, Refused]"}</code></td><td>what someone may do, from the middleware's context</td></tr>
						<tr><td><code>tables : List(Admin.Table)</code></td><td>the tables it shows, in order, with their options</td></tr>
						<tr><td><code>include : [Listed, All]</code></td><td><code>Listed</code>, unless it says: only those; <code>All</code>: every other table of the schema too, with no options, but the platform's own (<code>rocx_…</code>)</td></tr>
						<tr><td><code>schema : Str</code></td><td><code>"public"</code>, unless it says</td></tr>
						<tr><td><code>title : Str</code></td><td>what its pages call it: <code>"Database"</code>, unless it says</td></tr>
					</tbody>
				</table>
			</TableWrap>
			<p>
				<code>Admin.Table</code>, a table's options. Columns are named as the table names them; a name it doesn't
				have is ignored.
			</p>
			<TableWrap>
				<table>
					<thead><tr><th>Field</th><th>Unless it says</th><th>Is</th></tr></thead>
					<tbody>
						<tr><td><code>name : Str</code></td><td></td><td>the table</td></tr>
						<tr><td><code>label : Str</code></td><td>its name, readable: <code>book_tags</code> is Book tags</td><td>what the pages call it</td></tr>
						<tr><td><code>list : List(Str)</code></td><td>its first seven columns</td><td>the columns its list of rows shows, each with a filter</td></tr>
						<tr><td><code>search : List(Str)</code></td><td>no search box</td><td>where the search looks, for the text anywhere, in any case</td></tr>
						<tr><td><code>ordering : List(Str)</code></td><td>its key, newest first</td><td>the list's order: <code>["-published_at", "title"]</code></td></tr>
						<tr><td><code>read_only : List(Str)</code></td><td></td><td>shown on the form, never written</td></tr>
						<tr><td><code>hidden : List(Str)</code></td><td></td><td>never read, shown or written: a password's hash</td></tr>
						<tr><td><code>labels : List((Str, Str))</code></td><td>each column's name, readable</td><td><code>[("email", "Email address")]</code></td></tr>
						<tr><td><code>title : Str</code></td><td>its first text column that isn't its key</td><td>the column that names a row: its page's heading, and its name in a foreign key's select</td></tr>
						<tr><td><code>per_page : U64</code></td><td>50</td><td>rows a page</td></tr>
						<tr><td><code>widgets : List((Str, Widget.Kind))</code></td><td>the column's type's</td><td><code>[("body", LongText)]</code></td></tr>
						<tr><td><code>create : Bool</code>, <code>delete : Bool</code></td><td>yes</td><td>whether rows may be added, or deleted, here</td></tr>
					</tbody>
				</table>
			</TableWrap>

			<Heading id="widgets">Widgets</Heading>
			<p>Each column's input is chosen from its type, as the catalog says it (a domain's, its base type's):</p>
			<TableWrap>
				<table>
					<thead><tr><th>Column</th><th>Widget</th></tr></thead>
					<tbody>
						<tr><td>a foreign key to a table the admin shows</td><td>a select of that table's rows, by their title (an input, when there are more than 200), and a link to the row</td></tr>
						<tr><td>an enum; a check that it is one of a few values (<code>{"check (state in ('draft', 'published'))"}</code>)</td><td>a select of them</td></tr>
						<tr><td><code>boolean</code></td><td>a checkbox; a select of yes, no and null when it may be null</td></tr>
						<tr><td><code>smallint</code>, <code>integer</code>, <code>bigint</code></td><td>a number</td></tr>
						<tr><td><code>numeric</code>, <code>real</code>, <code>double precision</code></td><td>a decimal's input</td></tr>
						<tr><td><code>date</code>, <code>timestamp</code>, <code>timestamptz</code>, <code>time</code></td><td>the browser's pickers; a <code>timestamptz</code> in UTC</td></tr>
						<tr><td><code>json</code>, <code>jsonb</code></td><td>a text area, in a monospace font</td></tr>
						<tr><td><code>text</code>, <code>varchar(n)</code>, <code>uuid</code> and the rest</td><td>an input (<code>maxlength</code> for a <code>varchar(n)</code>), or a text area for a value of several lines; Postgres reads what is typed as the column's type</td></tr>
						<tr><td>a generated column, <code>bytea</code></td><td>shown, never written</td></tr>
					</tbody>
				</table>
			</TableWrap>
			<p>
				An empty input is null for a column that may be null, an empty text for text that may not, and, in a new
				row, the column's default for a column that has one.
			</p>

			<Heading id="how">How it works</Heading>
			<p>
				For each request, the admin reads the schema from Postgres's catalog (<code>pg_catalog</code>: four small
				statements), and turns its answer into tables (<code>Catalog.read</code>): their columns and types, which
				may be null, which have defaults, their primary keys, foreign keys, enums, and a check's values. A table
				or a column a request names is looked up there, among those the app lets it show, and only the
				catalog's names go into SQL, double-quoted. Every value is a parameter.
			</p>
			<Code title={"${Sources.admin_ident.file}, in part"} source={Sources.admin_ident.rocx} numbers={Bool.False} />
			<p>
				So a list is two statements, built by <code>Sql</code>: how many rows there are, then the page's. These are
				the ones for <code>{"?${AdminPreview.query}"}</code> in the admin above, as the package builds them while
				this page renders:
			</p>
			<Code title={"?${AdminPreview.query}"} source={AdminPreview.statements} plain numbers={Bool.False} />
			<p>
				Every statement answers with rows of one shape, each cell as text and whether it was null
				(<code>Catalog.Row</code>), so one <code>Db.all!</code> reads them all, whatever the table. A cell in a
				list is cut short in Postgres (to 81 characters, so the page can tell it was cut), never a key's.
			</p>
			<p>
				Pages are sent with <code>Content-Security-Policy: default-src 'none'</code> (no script, nothing loaded
				from elsewhere, forms sent only here), <code>X-Frame-Options: DENY</code> and
				<code>Cache-Control: no-store</code>, and every value in them is escaped.
			</p>

			<Heading id="modules">The modules</Heading>
			<p>
				<code>AdminPage</code> is the one with effects, and they are the ones the app hands it. The rest is plain
				Roc, on no platform: <code>roc test packages/admin/main.roc</code> runs their <code>expect</code>s.
			</p>
			<TableWrap>
				<table>
					<thead><tr><th>Module</th><th>What it does</th></tr></thead>
					<tbody>
						<tr><td><code>Admin</code></td><td>the options (<code>Config</code>, <code>Table</code>, <code>Access</code>), and what they mean for a table: <code>showing</code>, <code>listed</code>, <code>searchable</code>, <code>writable</code>, <code>title</code>, <code>ordering</code>, <code>widget</code></td></tr>
						<tr><td><code>AdminPage</code></td><td><code>respond!(request, context, config, effects)</code>: the access check, the token, the catalog, then the page or the form</td></tr>
						<tr><td><code>Catalog</code></td><td>the statements that read the schema, and <code>read</code>, their rows as tables</td></tr>
						<tr><td><code>Sql</code></td><td><code>ident</code>, <code>contains</code> (a search's or a filter's pattern), the conditions a list's rows meet, and the statements: <code>rows</code>, <code>count</code>, <code>one</code>, <code>insert</code>, <code>update</code>, <code>delete</code>, <code>titles</code></td></tr>
						<tr><td><code>Widget</code></td><td>a column's widget from its type (<code>of</code>), its input (<code>input</code>), what a form's text writes (<code>param</code>), whether it changed (<code>same</code>), a new row's value (<code>initial</code>)</td></tr>
						<tr><td><code>Listing</code></td><td>a list's search, filters, sort and page from the query, and back (<code>read</code>, <code>params</code>, <code>conditions</code>, <code>order</code>, <code>key</code>), each column's filter (<code>kind</code>) and the check of its values (<code>check</code>)</td></tr>
						<tr><td><code>Paging</code></td><td><code>of(total, size, asked)</code>, <code>offset</code>, <code>shows</code>, <code>links</code>: the first two pages, three each side of this one, the last two</td></tr>
						<tr><td><code>View</code></td><td>each page's HTML: <code>index_page</code>, <code>list_page</code>, <code>row_page</code>, <code>delete_page</code>, <code>problem_page</code></td></tr>
						<tr><td><code>Look</code></td><td>the document around a page, in a copy of the platform's admin look (<code>AdminHtml</code>'s stylesheet and helpers, light and dark), with the admin's own rules for forms, filters and pages, and the headers it is sent with</td></tr>
						<tr><td><code>Form</code></td><td>a query's or a form's fields, decoded, and links, encoded</td></tr>
					</tbody>
				</table>
			</TableWrap>

			<Heading id="status">Status</Heading>
			<p>
				Everything on this page works today, with the server platform as it is. The package's 172
				<code>expect</code>s test its plain modules. The whole admin was tried end to end in a scratch app
				against Postgres 18: an enum, a check, foreign keys, a key of two columns, generated and identity
				columns, every page and form, and the refusals. That test isn't in the repository's suite yet, since
				it needs a database.
			</p>
			<p>Two proposals would make it simpler:</p>
			<ul>
				<li>
					<strong>Server-only modules in packages</strong>: <code>rocx build</code> would copy a package's
					<code>server/</code> modules into the server's program only. Then <code>AdminPage</code> imports
					<code>Db</code> and <code>Random</code> itself, and a route's <code>respond!</code> is
					<code>AdminPage.respond!(request, context, admin)</code>, with no effects to hand it.
				</li>
				<li>
					<strong>The platform's admin look for apps</strong>: the server platform draws the jobs page with a
					module apps can't import yet (<code>AdminHtml</code>), so <code>Look</code> holds a copy of its
					stylesheet and helpers, which a test keeps the same as <code>AdminHtml</code>'s. Shared (the plugins
					design moves <code>AdminHtml</code> into this package), it would be one look in one place.
				</li>
			</ul>
			<p>Not yet:</p>
			<ul>
				<li>A list counts its rows (<code>count(*)</code>) and pages with <code>offset</code>: fine for thousands of rows, slow for millions.</li>
				<li>A foreign key in a list shows its value, linked to its row, not the row's title; its filter takes a value.</li>
				<li>A foreign key or a check over several columns gives no widget: each column is edited alone.</li>
				<li>No actions on several rows at once, and no history of who changed what.</li>
			</ul>
			<Note>
				The package's README and its proposals are in <code>packages/admin/</code>; the pages above are its
				<code>View</code>'s, and the code its own.
			</Note>
		</NoHydration>
	</>

<style>
	/* The admin's own pages, in frames: as wide as the text, which is narrow enough that the admin
	   lists its tables across the top, as on a phone. A book's form is taller than its frame, and
	   scrolls in it. */
	.admin-preview {
		margin: var(--space-5) 0;
	}

	.admin-preview iframe {
		display: block;
		width: 100%;
		height: 60rem;
		border: 1px solid var(--rule-strong);
		border-radius: var(--radius);
		background: #f4f6f7;
	}

	/* The list of books, whole: its filters in its table, its six rows and its count; on a phone,
	   its titles take two lines. */
	.admin-preview iframe.list {
		height: 52rem;
	}

	@media (max-width: 40rem) {
		.admin-preview iframe.list {
			height: 57rem;
		}
	}

	.admin-preview figcaption {
		margin-top: var(--space-2);
		color: var(--text-muted);
		font-size: var(--size-sm);
	}
</style>
